<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tongmenghui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1905">1905</a>–<a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Chinese Revolutionary Alliance, Revolutionary Alliance</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tongmenghui, or Revolutionary Alliance, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a> founded in Tokyo in August 1905 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> and other Chinese revolutionaries to overthrow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>. It united several revolutionary groups and organised a series of uprisings in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a> it merged with smaller parties in 1912 to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tongmenghui.jpg" alt="Tongmenghui" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lu Haodong · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naval_Jack_of_the_Republic_of_China.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tongmenghui of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a> and underground resistance movement, was founded in Tokyo by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-jiaoren">Song Jiaoren</a> and others on 20 August 1905. Its goal was to overthrow China's Qing dynasty. It was formed by the unification of Sun's Xingzhonghui (Revive China Society), the Guangfuhui (Restoration Society) and many other late-Qing revolutionary groups. Its members included Huang Xing, Li Zongren, Zhang Binglin, Chen Tianhua, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jingwei">Wang Jingwei</a>, Hu Hanmin, Tao Chengzhang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-yuanpei">Cai Yuanpei</a>, Li Shizeng, Zhang Renjie and Qiu Jin. In 1904 the movement had joined republican, nationalist and socialist objectives in a political goal: to expel the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> rulers, revive Zhonghua, establish a republic and distribute land equally among the people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1906 a branch was formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> after Sun's visit. Called the Nanyang branch, it served as the headquarters for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Its members included Tan Chor Lam, Teo Eng Hock and Chan Po-yin, who started the Chong Shing Chinese Daily Newspaper, with a first issue on 20 August 1907 and a daily distribution of 1,000 copies. Chan Po-yin raised over 30,000 yuan to buy military equipment and ship it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. From December 1906 to April 1908 the Qing government defeated seven Tongmenghui-led uprisings. Numerous members fled to Southeast Asia, and discontent with the leadership grew among the membership.</p><p>In 1909 the headquarters of the Nanyang branch moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a>, where Sun was based from July to December 1910. The 1910 Penang Conference there planned the Second Guangzhou Uprising. A meeting of Sun's supporters at Ipoh, at the villa of Teh Lay Seng, raised funds, and a drive across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a> collected 47,683 Straits Settlement dollars. After revolutionaries occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in November 1911 the Tongmenghui moved its headquarters there, and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> once the Provisional Government was established.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>At a general meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> on 20 January 1912, attended by thousands of members, Hu Hanmin moved on behalf of Sun that the oath be changed to overthrow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> government, consolidate the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and implement the Min Sheng Chu I. After the Republic was established the Tongmenghui became a political party on 3 March 1912 and elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> chairman, with Huang Xing and Li Yuanhung as vice-chairmen. It issued a provisional constitution of 34 articles. In May 1912 it moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, where it was the largest party in China, with branches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuhan">Wuhan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> and other cities, and about 550,000 members. In August 1912 it formed the nucleus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tongmenghui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Republic of China grew out of the revolutionary movement against the Qing Empire led by Sun Yat-sen, whose <b>Tongmenghui</b>, or Revolutionary Alliance, was founded in Tokyo in 1905.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The name “Three Principles of the People” comes from Feng Ziyou, who later claimed that while editing a Hong Kong <b>Tongmenghui</b> paper he shortened the phrase “promoting nationalism, democracy, and people's welfare” into the term.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/three-principles-of-the-people">Three Principles of the People · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
